## Summary

Makes pnpm the only tool needed for a Rust toolchain. It follows pnpm/pnpm#16741 (install a project's toolchain) and pnpm/pnpm#16748 (project-aware `cargo`/`rustc` shims). `rust` now names the Rust toolchain, as `node` names the Node.js runtime, instead of the npm package called `rust`, which is a wrapper that downloads one. The version is a Rust release channel: `1.95.0`, `1.95`, `stable`, `beta`, `nightly`, or a dated channel. A bare `rust` asks for `stable`.

- **`pnpm add rust@1.95`** in a project (refused through `-r`/`--filter`):
  - sets `channel` in the `rust-toolchain.toml` that governs the project (the nearest one at or above it in the workspace), or creates one in the project;
  - keeps the rest of the file, comments included;
  - restores the file if the add fails, because it is the task's declared metadata;
  - with `cargo.enabled`, also installs the toolchain and links it as `.pnpm/rust`.

  Nothing is written to `package.json`.
- **`pnpm add -g rust@stable`**:
  - installs the toolchain into the store the shims use;
  - writes the Rust tool shims, the same ones `pnpm shim add rust` writes;
  - records the channel in the global bin directory.

  The shims run that toolchain where nothing pins one, ahead of the next command on `PATH`. A project's own `rust-toolchain.toml` still wins. Whatever rustup decides goes to the next command on `PATH`: a `path` toolchain, `rustup override set`, `cargo +nightly`, `RUSTUP_TOOLCHAIN`, or a switched-off shim.
